AB  - This research report examines the privacy challenges posed by big data and smart city initiatives in Hong Kong. It highlights a significant gap between current governance mechanisms and public expectations regarding data privacy, particularly concerning government surveillance. The study analyzes two case studies: the "Multi-functional Smart Lampposts" pilot scheme and a facial recognition access system in a residential estate. It finds that existing regulations, like the Personal Data (Privacy) Ordinance, are insufficient to address new forms of personal data and the concerns surrounding surveillance states. The report recommends rebuilding public trust, enacting legislation to limit government data processing, mandating privacy impact assessments, improving data transparency, and establishing an independent body to oversee national security-related data collection.
